    {
      "id": "650cd2ed8d5d0e8abcad00755cebb56554549b50",
      "author": {
        "_account_id": 1146,
        "name": "Janitor Bot",
        "email": "janitor-gerrit@ddn.com",
        "username": "janitor-gerrit",
        "status": "building and testing"
      },
      "date": "2026-05-27 17:16:48.000000000",
      "message": "Patch Set 1:\n\nI attempted to fix the build errors present in this patch and succeeded. Yay! Now, keep in mind I barely have any idea what I am doing, so please doublecheck the changes I made to make sure they make sense.\n\nThis service is only provided in hopes to be useful and reduce latency from push to getting actual results in the face of trivial code errors\n\nHere's summary of my changes:\n## Fix Applied\n\n**What was fixed:**\nRemoved the `static` keyword from the `dot` and `dotdot` variable declarations in `lustre/lfsck/lfsck_lib.c` (lines 579-580).\n\n**Rationale:**\nThe compile error occurred because of a declaration mismatch:\n- The header file `lustre/lfsck/lfsck_internal.h` declares these variables as `extern const char dot[]` and `extern const char dotdot[]`\n- The patch incorrectly added `static` to these definitions in `lfsck_lib.c`, making them `static const char`\n\nIn C, when a variable is declared as `extern` in a header file, its definition in the source file must not be `static`. The `static` keyword gives the variable internal linkage (visible only within that translation unit), while `extern` declares that the variable has external linkage (visible to other translation units). These two are incompatible.\n\nThe fix changes the definitions from `static const char` back to `const char`, which provides external linkage and matches the `extern` declarations in the header file, allowing other source files that include `lfsck_internal.h` to access these variables.\n\n\nRequest ID: 908d10e2-7557-4bad-bd61-b90bdd93e6bb",
      "accounts_in_message": [],
      "_revision_number": 1
    },
